     "type": "application",
     "title": "An Evidence-Based Week",
     "content": "Build a single week that puts research behind four of the remaining rules at once. For reconnect with nature, schedule a twenty-minute outdoor walk, ideally somewhere with trees; research on shinrin-yoku, or forest bathing, has repeatedly found measurable reductions in cortisol and other stress markers after time spent in wooded settings compared to equivalent time in urban settings, a finding consistent with the older attention restoration theory proposed by environmental psychologists Rachel and Stephen Kaplan. For give thanks, write down three specific things each night before bed; a well-known study by Robert Emmons and Michael McCullough found that participants who kept a brief weekly gratitude journal reported significantly higher well-being than those who journaled about neutral or negative events, over just ten weeks. For live in the moment, try one meal a week entirely free of phones or screens; a large-scale smartphone-sampling study by Matthew Killingsworth and Daniel Gilbert, tracking real-time reports from thousands of people, found that minds wandered away from the present moment nearly half of the time and that this wandering, regardless of what it was about, predicted lower momentary happiness. For follow your ikigai, close the week by returning to the small ikigai log introduced earlier in this book. None of these four practices is complicated or expensive, and each has research support that exists entirely independent of this book, which is a useful test to apply before adopting any item from a popular self-help list.",
